Thanks for your reply - that is most insightful. 

What I don't see, however, is how the following fails to verify:

public class Array {
	[Rep] int[]! a;
	invariant a.Length==1;
	public Array()
	ensures exists{int k in (0:a.Length); a[k] == 0};//a4
	{
		a = new int[1];
		a[0] = 0;
		assert a[0] == 0;//a1
		assert a.Length==1;//a2
		assert exists{int k in (0:a.Length); a[k] == 0};//a3
	}
}

With the warning:
Method Array(), unsatisfied postcondition: exists{int k in (0:a.Length); a[k]==0}


I assume this is something to do with it being the constructor of the object, as this method does verify when it is not a constructor. Surely, though, this should verify as well? Am I missing something?

Many thanks,
Chris

(p.s. I am not sure why the line breaks are not coming up on the actual post, making this example difficult to read - apologies!)
